      "observation": "Contents of /Users/joaovictor/research-agents/Group-25-Research-Project/papers/PPLM/paper.pdf:\n 1 \nA Corporative Language Model for Protein–Protein Interaction, Binding Affinity, 1 \nand Interface Contact Prediction 2 \n 3 \nJun Liu1, Hungyu Chen2, Yang Zhang1,2,3,* 4 \n 5 \n1Cancer Science Institute of Singapore, National University of Singapore, 117599, Singapore. 6 \n2Department of Computer Science, School of Computing, National University of Singapore, 117417, 7 \nSingapore. 8 \n3Department of Biochemistry, Yong Loo Lin School of Medicine, National University of Singapore, 117596, 9 \nSingapore. 10 \n* Correspondence should be addressed to (zhang@nus.edu.sg) 11 \n 12 \nAbstract 13 \n 14 \nUnderstanding protein–protein interactions (PPIs) is crucial for deciphering cellular processes and 15 \nguiding therapeutic discovery. While recent protein language models have advanced sequence -based 16 \nprotein representation, most are designed for individual chains and fail to capture inherent PPI patterns. 17 \nWe introduce a novel Protein–Protein Language Model (PPLM) that jointly encodes interacting protein 18 \npair representations. Building on this foundation, we develop three specialized predictors—PPLM-PPI, 19 \nPPLM-Affinity, and PPLM -Contact—targeting binary interaction, binding affinity, and interface 20 \ncontact predictions, respectively. Large-scale experiments show that PPLM-PPI achieves state-of-the-21 \nart performance across different species on binary interaction prediction, while PPLM -Affinity 22 \noutperforms both ESM2 and structure -based methods on binding affinity modeling, particularly on 23 \nchallenging cases including  antibody–antigen and TCR–pMHC complexes. PPLM -Contact further 24 \nsurpasses existing PPI contact predictors on inter -protein contact prediction and interface residue 25 \nrecognition, including those deduced from cutting-edge complex structure predictions. Together, these 26 \nresults highlight the potential of co-represented language models to advance computational modeling 27 \nof PPIs. 28 \n  29 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 2 \nIntroduction 30 \nProtein–protein interactions (PPIs) are central to virtually all cellular processes, ranging from 31 \nsignal transduction and immune recognition to metabolic regulation and molecular assembly 1 32 \nUnderstanding PPIs involves three fundamental and interrelated questions: Do two proteins interact? 33 \nHow strong is their binding? And which residues mediate the interaction? These questions map directly 34 \nonto the key computational challenges of binary interaction prediction, binding affinity estimation, and 35 \ninterface residue identification2-4. 36 \nLarge-scale protein language models (PLMs), such as ESM5-7 and ProtBERT8, have demonstrated 37 \nstrong capabilities in learning protein sequence representations, driving progress in structure 38 \nprediction9, 10 , function annotation 11, protein design 12, and mutational effect estimation 13. Although 39 \nPLMs have been widely applied in PPI -related studies14-20, these models are inherently trained on 40 \nsingle-protein sequences and lack the ability to model inter -protein relationships. For protein 41 \ninteraction prediction, for example, methods such as D -SCRIPT15, Topsy-Turvy21, and ESMDNN -42 \nPPI22 leverage either LSTM-based encoders or ESM2 -derived embeddings from single -sequence for 43 \nbinary classification, yet struggle to capture interface -level context. In the task of binding affinity 44 \nprediction, most PLM -based approaches are designed for protein -ligand targets 23, 24 , with existing 45 \nmethods for protein–protein binding affinity prediction still depending heavily on complex structural 46 \nmodels25-29, highlighting a key opportunity for developing scalable, structure -free solutions based on 47 \nsequence-derived representations.  For inter -protein contact prediction, most methods are built on 48 \nmultiple sequence alignments (MSAs) 30-33. While methods such as GLINTER 34, CDPred 19, and 49 \nDeepInter20 employ ESM-MSA-1b35 to extract both intra- and inter-protein features, their performance 50 \nis tightly coupled to the quality and availability of paired MSAs, as well as the fact that ESM -MSA-51 \n1b has been pretrained solely on monomeric sequences. These limitations, shared across all three major 52 \nPPI-related tasks, underscore the need for a unified paired protein language model that can directly 53 \nlearn inter-protein relationships from sequences. 54 \nTo address these limitations, we introduce PPLM, a novel protein–protein language model 55 \nspecifically designed to learn contextual and relational features from paired protein sequences. To 56 \nmodel inter -protein relationships accurately, we design a new hybrid attention mechanism that 57 \nemployes Rotary embeddings 36 for intra-protein positional encoding  and non-positional embedding 58 \nfor inter-protein residue pairs to avoid spurious spatial priors. Learnable inter-protein attention weights 59 \nand an inter -protein attention mask are then introduced to enhance the learning of inter -protein 60 \nattention and explicitly separate intra- from inter-protein dependencies. To support large-scale training, 61 \nwe construct a comprehensive paired sequence dataset comprising over 3.3 million high -quality 62 \nprotein pairs curated from the Protein Data Bank (PDB) 37 and STRING 38 databases. This dataset 63 \nallows robust training of PPLM to capture interaction-aware representations. 64 \nBuilding upon PPLM, we develop three task -specific methods for key PPI -related challenges: 65 \nPPLM-PPI for binary interaction prediction, PPLM -Affinity for binding affinity estimation, and 66 \nPPLM-Contact for inter-protein contact prediction and interface residue identification . Across these 67 \ntasks, PPLM consistently outperforms existing methods, with notable gains in challenging scenarios 68 \nsuch as antibody–antigen and TCR–pMHC complexes. In inter -protein contact -map prediction, 69 \nPPLM-Contact surpasses current state -of-the-art structure modelling approaches, including 70 \nAlphaFold2.339, AlphaFold340, and DMFold41. These results underscore PPLM’s potential as a unified 71 \nfoundation for advancing computational PPI studies. 72 \n 73 \nResults 74 \nPPLM consists of four distinct pipelines designed for the core language model and its downstream 75 \napplications. In the primary language modeling pipeline  (Figure 1A ), input sequences are 76 \nindependently tokenized with begin and end tokens, then concatenated to form the full-size encoding. 77 \nA specialized attention mechanism is then employed to capture both inter - and intra-protein residue 78 \ninteractions in parallel. The resulting embeddings and attention matrix from PPLM can be utilized  in 79 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 3 \nvarious ways for PPI-related research, offering flexible features for a range of downstream tasks. 80 \nTo illustrate the versatility of PPLM,  we developed three task -specific models targeting major 81 \nchallenges in PPI studies. For PPI prediction ( Figure 1B ), PPLM generates interaction features 82 \nincluding intra -protein embeddings, intra -protein attention maps, and inter-protein attention maps. 83 \nThese are processed independently and then concatenated into a single representation, which is passed 84 \nthrough a multilayer perceptron (MLP) with sigmoid activation to predict the probability of interaction 85 \nbetween the input sequence pairs. For binding affinity prediction (Figure 1C), the final layer of PPLM 86 \nis fine-tuned on a small, high-quality dataset of experimentally measured affinities. The full-sequence 87 \nembeddings of the receptor and ligand  proteins are aggregated using max pooling and then passed 88 \nthrough linear layers to estimate their binding strength.  Finally, f or inter-protein contact-map 89 \nprediction (Figure 1D), the inter-protein attention matrix from PPLM is integrated with MSA-derived 90 \nfeatures and monomer distance maps to capture both evolutionary and structural information. A 91 \ndedicated inter-protein transformer, composed of three specialized modules  designed to update inter-92 \nprotein representations, is trained to predict the inter-protein contacts and identify interface residues  93 \nbetween the input monomer proteins. 94 \nTo rigorously evaluate PPLM’s performance, we compiled independent benchmark datasets for 95 \neach downstream task: (1) For language model evaluation, 1,194 homodimer and 1,306 heterodimer 96 \nprotein sequence pairs were collected from the PDB 37, along with 2,500 high -confidence sequence 97 \npairs from STRING 38; (2) For protein–protein interaction prediction, 22,000 positive and 216,183 98 \nnegative PPI samples  spanning 5 species  were used 15; (3) For binding affinity prediction, 12,062 99 \ninteraction samples from 3,032 distinct PDB entries were used in five -fold cross-validation setting27; 100 \nand (4) For inter -protein contact prediction and interface residue identification, 300 homodimer and 101 \n99 heterodimer proteins from DeepInter42, as well as 43 homodimer and 20 heterodimer proteins from 102 \nthe Critical Assessment of Structure Prediction (CASP13-16) experiments. Both the training and test 103 \ndatasets are made publicly available at https://zhanggroup.org/PPLM/benchmark.html. 104 \nDetailed methodological descriptions and dataset specifications are provided in the Methods 105 \nsection. 106 \n 107 \nPPLM improves language modeling of protein pairs 108 \nPerplexity serves as a task -independent and objective metric for evaluating the predictive  109 \nperformance of protein language models, capturing the model’s uncertainty in predicting amino acid 110 \nresidues. Lower perplexity values correspond to higher confidence and accuracy, with a score of 1 111 \nindicating perfect prediction and value  ~20 representing near-random performance. In this study, we 112 \nfirst employed perplexity to benchmark the performance of PPLM against ESM2 6, a state-of-the-art 113 \nsingle-sequence protein language model trained on large -scale individual protein sequences without 114 \nexplicitly modeling inter-protein context, on the validation dataset of PPLM. 115 \nThe left panel of Figure 2A presents the perplexity results under a random masking strategy, where 116 \neach residue from both input sequences was sequentially masked to compute pseudo perplexity. PPLM 117 \nachieves average perplexities of 3.00 and 4.01 for protein sequence pairs from PDB and STRING, 118 \nrespectively, representing reductions of 23.5% and 22.6% compared to ESM2 (3.92 and 5.18).  These 119 \nimprovements are statistically significant, with Wilcoxon signed-rank test p-values < 1E-300 for both 120 \ndatasets. The median perplexities are 1.83 (PDB) and 3.88 (STRING), which are 22.5% and 20.3% 121 \nlower than those of ESM2 (2.36 and 4.87). Notably, the observed median perplexities of PPLM are 122 \nconsiderably lower than the corresponding means, indicating that PPLM performs well on the majority 123 \nof samples, with a small number of challenging outliers contributing to higher perplexity values. 124 \nFigure 2B further presents a head-to-head comparison of perplexity under the random masking, where 125 \nPPLM outperforms ESM2 in 4,491 out of 5000 cases (89.8%), while ESM2 achieves lower perplexity 126 \nin only 419 (8.4%) cases. 127 \nTo further examine PPLM’s sensitivity to interfacial residues, we evaluated perplexity under an 128 \ninterface masking strategy using PDB -derived protein pairs. In this setup, only residues known to 129 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 4 \nparticipate in protein–protein interfaces were masked. Here, t wo masking modes were defined for 130 \nheteromeric pairs: the Both mode, which masks interface residues from both sequences, and the Single 131 \nmode, which masks interface residues from only one sequence. For homomeric pairs, due to sequence 132 \nidentity, only the Both mode was applicable.  133 \nAs shown in Figure 2A, PPLM achieves an average perplexity of 8.79 and a median 7.59 in the 134 \n‘Both’ mode, significantly lower than those of ESM2 (10.60 and 10.01), with a Wilcoxon signed-rank 135 \ntest p-value of 5.96E-251. In the ‘Single’ mode, PPLM again outperforms ESM2, with the average and 136 \nmedian perplexities of 8.24 and 6.34, compared to 10.72 and 10.36 for ESM2 ( with p-value = 4.35E-137 \n237). Figure 2C further displays the head -to-head comparison of perplexity under the two modes, 138 \nwhere PPLM demonstrated a lower perplexity than ESM2 on 79.3% cases in the ‘Both’ mode and 78.8% 139 \ncases in the ‘Single’ mode. 140 \nInterestingly, in heteromeric pairs, both the average and median perplexities of PPLM under the 141 \n‘Single’ mode were lower than those under the ‘Both’ mode, an effect not observed with ESM2. This 142 \nsuggests that PPLM is capable of capturing meaningful inter -protein interface signals and effectively 143 \nleveraging the partner protein’s context to recover masked interface residues. 144 \nOverall, these results highlight PPLM’s improved sensitivity to residue -level interactions at 145 \nprotein–protein interfaces. This advantage is primarily attributed to its incorporative training  146 \nframework for paired sequences , augmented by a hybrid attention network architecture  which 147 \nexplicitly captures intra - and inter -protein interaction  patterns. Such capabilities offer important 148 \nbenefits for downstream applications, including interface residue identification and structure -guided 149 \ninterface design, such as antibody CDR optimization. 150 \n 151 \nPPLM-PPI outperforms existing methods in binary protein interaction prediction 152 \nTo examine the effectiveness of PPLM-PPI on protein–protein interaction predictions, we tested it 153 \non five PPI datasets across five species, including M. musculus , D. melanogaster , C. elegans , S. 154 \ncerevisiae, and E. coli . PPLM-PPI was compared against three  state-of-the-art LM -based PPI 155 \npredictors, including ESMDNN-PPI22, D-SCRIPT15, and Topsy-Turvy21. To ensure reproducibility, all 156 \nmethods were installed and executed locally, except for ESMDNN-PPI, which lacks publicly available 157 \nsource code, and we reproduced it locally based on the implementation details provided in its original 158 \npublication. 159 \nThe area under the precision -recall curve (AUPRC) is a performance metric that emphasizes the 160 \nmodel’s ability to correctly identify positive samples, which is particularly challenging for the 161 \nimbalanced class distributions  such as genome -wide PPI identifications. Figure 3A summarizes the 162 \nAUPRC of PPLM-PPI against the three control methods. PPLM-PPI achieves AUPRC scores of 0.895, 163 \n0.880, 0.854, 0.708, and 0.759 on the five test species, corresponding to improvements of 11.7%, 6.7%, 164 \n7.4%, 18.8%, and 11.3% over ESMDNN -PPI; 54.3%, 59.1%, 55.8%, 74.8%, and 32.0% over D -165 \nSCRIPT; and 40.9%, 35.6%, 38.6%, 63.9%, and 86.9% over Topsy-Turvy, respectively. These results 166 \ndemonstrate the superior precision-recall performance of PPLM-PPI in distinguishing true PPIs across 167 \ndiverse species. 168 \nTo test the balanced ability for recognizing both positive and negative PPI samples, Figure 3B 169 \nreports the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) results, where PPLM-PPI 170 \nachieves the highest AUROC score across all species among all methods. In Figure 3C, we further list 171 \nthe precision -recall curves of all methods  across the five species datasets. PPLM-PPI consistently 172 \noutperforms other methods across most recall thresholds. Meanwhile, the PPLM -PPI curves are 173 \nnoticeably smoother than those of other methods, reflecting its  strong stability and predictive 174 \nconsistency, particularly in high -precision or high -recall regions. Table S1 provides a summary of 175 \ndifferent metrics, including precision, recall, accuracy, F1 -score, AUROC, and AUPRC, across all 176 \nspecies, confirming the overall superiors of PPLM-PPI over all control methods in nearly every metric. 177 \nThis consistent robustness across diverse species and evaluation metrics underscores the potential of 178 \nPPLM-PPI for real-world, high-confidence PPI discovery applications. 179 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 5 \nTo elucidate the contributions of different components within PPLM -PPI, we conducted ablation 180 \nexperiments by replacing the pooling module and selectively removing specific features generated by 181 \nPPLM. These experiments were designed to assess how each component impacts interaction prediction 182 \nperformance ( Tables S2 and S3 ). As shown in Figure S1A, the choice of pooling strategy had a 183 \nsubstantial impact on model performance. Among the single pooling approaches, the mean pooling 184 \nconsistently delivered the best results, while min pooling produced the worst performance across 185 \nspecies due to its sensitivity to weak or noisy signals. Notably, a combined strategy integrating both 186 \nmean and max pooling achieved the most robust and effective overall performance. We further 187 \ninvestigated the relative contributions of the three feature types: embedding, intra -protein attention, 188 \nand inter -protein attention ( Figure S1B). Among them, embedding features contributed most 189 \nsubstantially to predictive performance, likely due to their ability to capture both intra - and inter-190 \nprotein contextual information. Incorporating all three feature types resulted in the highest overall 191 \naccuracy, underscoring the complementary strengths of attention -based and embedding -based 192 \nrepresentations in modeling protein interaction propensity. These findings highlight the importance of 193 \nboth robust pooling strategies and multi -view feature integration in capturing the complex 194 \ndeterminants of protein–protein interactions. In particular, the synergy between embedding and 195 \nattention-based representations proved critical for achieving high predictive accuracy in cross-species 196 \nPPI tasks. 197 \n 198 \nPPLM-Affinity achieves superior performance in protein binding affinity prediction 199 \nWe assessed the performance of PPLM-Affinity on the recently constructed PPB-Affinity dataset27, 200 \na large and comprehensive benchmark for protein–protein binding affinity prediction. Evaluation was 201 \nconducted using five-fold cross-validation, following the original protocol defined for the benchmark. 202 \nTo examine the effectiveness of language models in this task, we implemented ESM2-Affinity, where 203 \nthe receptor and ligand sequences were concatenated and used as input to a model trained with the 204 \nsame architecture and fine-tuning procedure as PPLM-Affinity. In addition, we also compared PPLM-205 \nAffinity with the PPB-Affinity model, which was trained by Liu et al27 using the original PPB-Affinity 206 \ndataset built on structural features of the protein–protein complexes. 207 \nThe left panel of Figure 3D presents the Pearson correlation coefficient (PCC) and Spearman 208 \ncorrelation coefficient (SRCC) between the experimentally measured and predicted binding affinities 209 \n(ΔG) for PPLM -Affinity, ESM2 -Affinity, and PPB -Affinity across the entire dataset. The 210 \ncorresponding average values are summarized in Table S4. These two metrics are complementary: 211 \nPCC quantifies the strength of linear relationships between predicted and experimental values, while 212 \nSRCC assesses the strength of monotonic relationships based on their rank order. PPLM -Affinity 213 \nachieves a PCC of 0.764 and an SRCC of 0.752, representing improvements of 14.2% and 15.9% over 214 \nESM2-Affinity (0.669 and 0.649), and 8.99% and 8.83% over the structure-based PPB-Affinity model 215 \n(0.701 and 0.691), respectively. 216 \nFigure S2A further illustrates the distribution of prediction errors relative to experimental ΔG 217 \nvalues. All three models show  approximately Gaussian error distributions centered around zero , 218 \nsuggesting stable predictive behavior with limited systematic bias . Notably, the error distribution of 219 \nPPLM-Affinity is more tightly centered around zero, with the lowest standard deviation 𝜎 = 1.898, 220 \ncompared to 2.179 by ESM2 -Affinity and 2.083 by PPB -Affinity, indicating smaller and more 221 \nconsistent prediction errors across the dataset. 222 \nFigures 3E and 3F display scatter plots of predicted versus experimental ΔG values for PPLM -223 \nAffinity and ESM2 -Affinity, respectively. While both models exhibit positive correlations with 224 \nexperimental measurements, consistent with Figure 3D, the predictions from PPLM-Affinity lie closer 225 \nto the diagonal line, reflecting greater accuracy. This is further supported by a lower RMSE (1.898 vs. 226 \n2.179), indicating improved predictive precision and reduced variance. Given that both models share 227 \nthe same architecture and training pipeline, the substantial gains achieved by PPLM-Affinity highlight 228 \nthe advantage of PPLM as a language model specifically optimized for protein–protein interactions. 229 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 6 \nFurthermore, its superior performance over the structure -based PPB-Affinity model underscores its 230 \ncapability to accurately infer binding affinities directly from sequence, providing a scalable and 231 \nstructure-independent alternative for affinity prediction. 232 \nThe middle and right panels of Figure 3D further evaluate performance on two biologically 233 \nsignificant PPI subgroups: antibody–antigen and TCR–pMHC, which are critical for immunological 234 \nrecognition and therapeutic development 43, 44 . On the antibody–antigen subset, PPLM -Affinity 235 \nachieves PCC and SRCC values of 0.548 and 0.5 53, representing improvements over 95% compared 236 \nto ESM2-Affinity (0.280 and 0.281), and 66.6% and 85.6% over PPB-Affinity (0.329 and 0.298). On 237 \nthe TCR–pMHC subset, PPLM -Affinity achieves PCC and SRCC values of 0.565 and 0.505, 238 \ncorresponding to gains of 5 4.0% and 60.3% over ESM2-Affinity (0.367 and 0.315), and 27.5% and 239 \n11.7% over PPB-Affinity (0.443 and 0.452), respectively. 240 \nFigures S2B/D and S2C/E display scatter plots of predicted versus experimental ΔG values for 241 \nthe antibody–antigen and TCR–pMHC subsets, respectively. In both cases, PPLM -Affinity produces 242 \na more pronounced upward trend, with predictions tightly clustered around the diagonal, indicating a 243 \nstronger alignment with experimental values. In contrast, ESM2-Affinity shows a more diffuse pattern, 244 \nwith notably higher RMSE values  (1.683 and 1. 450 for antibody–antigen and TCR –pMHC, 245 \nrespectively, compared to 1. 924 and 1. 690 for PPLM -Affinity). These results highlight PPLM -246 \nAffinity’s improved robustness and accuracy in modeling both general and immunologically complex 247 \ninteractions, suggesting its potential utility in antibody engineering, TCR optimization, and other 248 \nsequence-based immunotherapeutic design tasks. 249 \n 250 \nPPLM-Contact outperforms existing methods in inter-protein contact prediction  251 \nThe performance of PPLM-Contact is evaluated on four benchmark test sets: Homodimer300 and 252 \nHeterodimer99 from DeepInter 20, and CASP_Homodimer43 and CASP_Heterodimer20 collected 253 \nfrom CASP13-16 experiments. It was compared against four state-of-the-art methods: DeepInter20, 254 \nCDPred19, GLINTER34, and DeepHomo2.031. To access reliability, we measured inter-protein contact 255 \nprecision at various cutoffs of top 1, 10, 50, 𝐿/10, 𝐿/5, and 𝐿 inter-protein contact precision sorted by 256 \nthe confidence score, where L is the length of the shorter protein chain in the complex. A summary of 257 \nthe result is provided in Supplementary Table S5 –6, with detailed outcomes available in 258 \nSupplementary Data 1 –2. To ensure reproducibility, all comparison methods were installed and 259 \nexecuted locally. 260 \nFigure 4A presents the top L contact precision of each method on homodimer test sets, using 261 \nexperimental or AlphaFold2-predicted monomer structures. On Homodimer300 with experimental 262 \nmonomer structures, PPLM -Contact achieves a top L contact precision of 77.8%, outperforming 263 \nDeepInter (68.9%), CDPred (63.0%), DeepHomo2.0 (51.4%), and GLINTER (34.5%) by margins of 264 \n12.9%, 23.5%, 51.4%, and 125.5%, respectively. These improvements are statistically significant, with 265 \np-values of 3.65 E-21, 5.58 E-31, 3.17 E-43, and 1.30 E-47 based on  the Wilcoxon signed -rank test.  266 \nWhen using AlphaFold2-predicted monomer structures, PPLM-Contact maintains strong performance 267 \nwith a top L precision of 66.6%, outperforming the second-best method, DeepInter, by 10.4% (p-value 268 \n= 4.07E-13). A similar trend is observed on the CASP_Homodimer43 test set , where PPLM-Contact 269 \nachieves a precision of 65.2% when using experimental monomer structures, outperforming DeepInter 270 \nby 6.0%, CDPred by 46.8%, DeepHomo2.0 by 90.6%, and GLINTER by 157.7%. When using 271 \nAlphaFold2-predicted monomer structures, the corresponding improvements are 13.0%, 46.3%, 272 \n68.2%, and 140.0%, respectively. 273 \nFigure 4B further lists the top L contact precision of each method on heterodimer test sets. On the 274 \nHeterodimer99 test set, PPLM-Contact achieves a top L contact precision of 48.0% using experimental 275 \nmonomer structures and 43.5% with AlphaFold2-predicted monomers. These represent improvements 276 \nof 37.1% and 32.2% over DeepInter, 91.2% and 58.2% over CDPred,  and 177.5% and 158.9% over 277 \nGLINTER, respectively. All improvements are statistically significant supported by Wilcoxon signed-278 \nrank test s (p -values reported in Table S5). On the CASP_Heterodimer20 test set, PPLM -Contact 279 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 7 \nachieves a top L contact precision of 46.8% with experimental monomer structures and 40.6% with 280 \nAlphaFold2-predicted monomers, which are also significantly higher than that of all control methods 281 \n(Left panel of Figure 4B). 282 \nFigures 4C provide head-to-head comparisons between PPLM-Contact and other methods across 283 \nthe four benchmark test sets using experimental monomer structures. On the Homodimer300 test set, 284 \nPPLM-Contact achieves the highest top L contact precision among all methods on  239 out of 300 285 \nproteins (79.7%) and leads on 30 out of 43 proteins  in the  CASP_Homodimer43 test set . For the 286 \nHeterodimer99 test set, PPLM-Contact outperforms DeepInter, CDPred, and GLINTER on 54, 61, and 287 \n72 out of 99 proteins, respectively, while underperforming only on 28, 22, and 15 proteins, and 288 \nshowing equal precision on the remaining cases. On CASP_Heterodimer20  test set, PPLM-Contact 289 \nsurpasses the three methods on 16, 14, and 16 proteins, respectively, with inferior performance 290 \nobserved on only 3, 5, and 2 cases.  Figure S3 list head-to-head comparisons based on AlphaFold2 -291 \npredicted monomer structures, showing a similar trend which PPLM-Contact outperforms the control 292 \nmethods in the majority of cases. 293 \nTo evaluate the contribution of individual feature types, we trained three ablated variants of PPLM-294 \nContact, each excluding one key component while keeping all other settings unchanged : (i) PPLM-295 \nContact w/o MSA, which excludes MSA-derived features (PSSM, DCA, and ESM-MSA-1b outputs); 296 \n(ii) PPLM-Contact w/o PPLM, which removes inter-protein attention features generated by  PPLM; 297 \nand (iii) PPLM -Contact w/o Mdist, which omits  monomer distance maps extracted from either 298 \nexperimental or AlphaFold-predicted monomer structures. Figure 4D illustrates the changes in top 1 299 \nto L contact precisions for these variants  on Homodimer300 and Heterodimer99 test sets using 300 \nexperimental monomer structures. The removal of monomer distance maps led to the most substantial 301 \ndrop, the top L contact precision decrease by 31.2% and 30.8%  for homodimers and heterodimers, 302 \nrespectively, underscoring the critical role of monomer geometry. Excluding MSA-derived features 303 \nled to a  precision drop of 9.7% and 18.7%, reflecting the valuable contribution of co-evolutionary 304 \ninformation. Conversely, incorporating PPLM-generated features boosted top L contact precision by 305 \n6.6% and 6.1%,  demonstrating PPLM’s effectiveness in capturing essential inter-protein geometric 306 \nrelationships. The results based on AlphaFold2-predicted monomer structures are also listed in Table 307 \nS7 and Datasets 3–4, which demonstrate similar trend as observed for that on experimental monomer 308 \nstructures. These comparisons emphasize the complementary nature and essential roles of each feature 309 \ntype in accurate inter-protein contact prediction.  310 \nAs an illustration, Figure 4E-F showcase an example of the EF-hand domain of human RASEF  311 \n(PDB ID: 2PMY), which consists of two identical protein chains, each with 91 amino acids, 72 of 312 \nwhich have experimentally resolved structures. When using only MSA-derived features and monomer 313 \nstructures, similar to most existing methods, PPLM-Contact w/o PPLM yields largely incorrect top N 314 \ncontact predictions (where N equals the number of ground -truth contacts), resulting in a low contact 315 \nprecision of 16.7% (20.8% for top  L contacts). In contrast, replacing the MSA -derived features with 316 \nPPLM-generated features allows the PPLM -Contact w/o MSA to correctly identify 201 out of 257 317 \nground-truth contacts (78.2%), achieving 100% precision for top  L contacts. When combining both 318 \nMSA and PPLM features, the full PPLM -Contact model further improves top  N contact precision to 319 \n81.3%, while maintaining 100% precision for top  L contacts. The result of this example highlights 320 \nagain the effectiveness of PPLM in capturing underlying geometric patterns and interface-level spatial 321 \nrelationships, which are crucial for accurate inter-protein contact prediction.  322 \n 323 \nPPLM-Contact enhances interface residue recognition 324 \nIn addition to protein–protein contact prediction, recognizing what residues participate in the PPIs 325 \nis of practical usefulness in determining complex formation, signaling, and biological activi ty45. In 326 \nFigure 5, we report the precision of PPLM-Contact for interface residue recognitions, compared to the 327 \nsame set of four control methods, based on experimentally determined monomer structures across 343 328 \nhomodimer and 119 heterodimer test proteins (see Figure S4 for results using AlphaFold2-predicted 329 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 8 \nmonomer structures). Here, we defined interface residues as those whose heavy atoms are within 8 Å 330 \nof any heavy atom from the opposite chain in the experimental structure , where only the top M 331 \npredictions are assessed with M being the number of ground -truth interface residues for each target , 332 \nregardless of their specific contact partners (Supplementary Data 5–6). 333 \nAs shown in Figure 5A, PPLM-Contact achieves the highest interface recognition precision of 334 \n0.860 for homodimers and 0. 729 for heterodimers, exceeding DeepInter (0. 810 and 0.633) by 6. 3% 335 \nand 15.2%, CDPred (0.805 and 0.607) by 6.9% and 20.2%, DeepHomo2.0 (0.720) by 19.5%, and 336 \nGLINTER (0.651 and 0.498) by 32.2% and 46.3% , respectively. Wilcoxon signed-rank tests confirm 337 \nthe statistical significance of these performance improvements (Table S8). Figure 5B further provides 338 \nhead-to-head comparisons of identification precision between PPLM-Contact and each control method 339 \non homodimer and heterodimer proteins, respectively . PPLM -Contact outperforms competing 340 \nmethods for 74.6%–92.7% homodimer proteins and 73.9%–90.8% heterodimer proteins. Compared to 341 \nthe second -best method ( DeepInter), PPLM -Contact improves precision by more than 0.2 in 57 342 \nproteins (11.5%), while the corresponding numbers are 91 proteins (19.7%) for CDPred, 94 proteins 343 \n(27.4%) for DeepHomo2.0, and 230 proteins (49.8%) for GLINTER. 344 \nFigures 5C showcases an example of the Crystal structure of DNMT3A-DNMT3L complex (PDB 345 \nID: 4U7P), which consists of two distinct chains with experimentally resolved structures for 426 and 346 \n197 amino acids, respectively. Figure 5D visually highlights the interface residues identified by each 347 \nmethod on the complex structure, while Figure 5E further compares the positions of both ground-truth 348 \nand predicted interface residues. It was found that PPLM-Contact successfully recognized 40 out of 349 \n43 (93.0%) interface residues in chain A and 40 out of 44 (90.9%) interface residue in chain B, 350 \ndemonstrating a substantial improvement over the control methods. In comparison, DeepInter 351 \nidentified 4 (9.3%) and 26 (59.1%) interface residues, CDPred identified 29 (67.4%) and 31 (70.5%), 352 \nand GLINTER missed all interface residues in chain A and only identified 8 (18.2%) interface residues 353 \nin chain B . Detailed analyses show that all the control methods showed an overprediction of the 354 \ninterface residues on the regions close to N-terminal for chain A, while for chain B the overpredictions 355 \nare mostly located on C -terminal region. In contrast, PPLM -Contact demonstrated a much more 356 \nbalanced interface residue predictions along both chains A and B, result ing in an excellent overall 357 \nprecision of 0.92 for chain s A and B which is significantly higher than that by DeepInter (0.35), 358 \nCDPred (0.69), and GLINTER (0.09), respectively. 359 \nThese results demonstrate that PPLM-Contact achieves superior performance in interface residue 360 \nrecognition compared to existing methods. This improvement can be primarily attributed to two key 361 \ninnovations. First, PPLM effectively captures the PPI signals that complement co -evolutionary 362 \ninformation from MSA, providing reliable features for the deep learning model. Second, the inter -363 \nprotein specific transformer network, designed with a parallel architecture, enables efficient learning 364 \nof geometric interactions between residues across protein interfaces. 365 \n 366 \nEnhancing LM-based interface contact prediction with predicted complex structures 367 \nRecent advances in protein complex structure prediction have enabled access to highly accurate 368 \nmodels of protein assemblies, offering valuable insights into interfacial organization. These predicted 369 \nstructures provide an opportunity to inform  interface prediction with rich geometric context . To 370 \nleverage this progress, we developed PPLM-Contact2, an enhanced version of PPLM -Contact that 371 \nincorporates inter -protein distance maps extracted from complex structur e models  predicted by 372 \nAlphaFold2.339, AlphaFold340, and DMFold 41 as additional features. We evaluated PPLM -Contact2 373 \nagainst the state-of-the-art structure-based methods on inter-protein contact prediction (Supplementary 374 \nTable S9–11, Data 7–9).  375 \nFigure 6A presents the top L contact precisions of PPLM -Contact2 in comparison with 376 \nAlphaFold2.3, AlphaFold3, and DMFold  across four benchmark test sets: Homodimer300, 377 \nHeterodimer99, CASP_Homodimer43, and CASP_Heterodimer20. PPLM -Contact2 achieves 378 \nprecisions of 86.4%, 89.9%, 75.4%, and 79.1% on these test sets , respectively , which  represent 379 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 9 \nconsistent improvements over AlphaFold2.3 by 4.0%, 4.8%, 13.5%, and 28.4%, over AlphaFold3 by 380 \n8.1%, 4.4%, 17.0%, and 15.7%, and over DMFold by 5.5%, 8.3%, 7.3%, and 7.7%. The improvements 381 \nare statistically significant, as confirmed by Wilcoxon signed-rank tests (p-values reported in Table 382 \nS9). Figure 6C–E illustrate head-to-head comparisons between PPLM-Contact2 and the structure-383 \nbased methods across all 462 test proteins, where PPLM-Contact2 achieves a higher precision on 408 384 \nproteins (88.3%) compared to AlphaFold2.3, on 400 proteins (86.6%) compared to AlphaFold, and on 385 \n412 proteins (89.2%) compared to DMFold.  Figure S5 presents the distributions of precision, recall, 386 \nand F1-score for inter -protein contact prediction across all test proteins. Compared to the complex 387 \nstructure modeling methods, PPLM-Contact2 demonstrates a clear shift toward higher score ranges in 388 \nall three metrics . It achieves an F1 -score of 0.780, surpassing AlphaFold2.3 (0.748), AlphaFold3 389 \n(0.739), and DMFold (0.743) by 4. 3%, 5.5%, and 5.0%, respectively, highlighting its robustness and 390 \nconsistency in predicting inter-protein contacts. 391 \nWe further evaluated the ability of these methods to identify interface residues. Figure 6B presents 392 \nthe precision of interface residue identification by PPLM -Contact2 and the structure-based methods. 393 \nAgain, PPLM-Contact2 achieves the highest average  and median precision for both homodimer and 394 \nheterodimer proteins.  Specifically, its average and median precision reached 0.897 and 0.9 62 for 395 \nhomodimers, and 0.904 and 0.945 for heterodimers, respectively. Wilcoxon signed-rank tests confirm 396 \nthat these improvements over the control methods are statistically significant (Table S11).  Figure S6 397 \nillustrate head-to-head comparisons of precision between PPLM -Contact2 and the control methods 398 \nacross all proteins, highlighting PPLM-Contact2’s superior performance on most proteins. 399 \nThese results indicate that, despite the remarkable progress in end -to-end complex structure 400 \nprediction, additional gains can be obtained by incorporating structural models with paired language 401 \nmodel-based pipelines to enhance inter-chain contact map and PPI binding sites predictions. This 402 \nintegrated information can be further used as spatial constraints to guide quaternary structure assembly 403 \nsimulations for more accurate protein complex structure predictions. 404 \n 405 \nDiscussion 406 \nComputational modeling of PPIs is essential for elucidating cellular mechanisms and accelerating 407 \ntherapeutic discovery. While protein language models offer a scalable solution for high-throughput PPI 408 \nanalysis, current models often fall short in capturing the inter-protein context and interaction-specific 409 \nfeatures critical for accurate prediction. To address these limitations, we developed PPLM, a protein–410 \nprotein language model that learns directly from paired sequences through a novel attention 411 \narchitecture, enabling explicit modeling of inter-protein context. Built upon PPLM, we introduced 412 \nthree task-specific downstream methods , PPLM-PPI, PPLM-Affinity and PPLM -Contact, targeting 413 \nthree progressively key PPI tasks : binary interaction prediction, binding affinity estimation, and 414 \ninterface contact prediction (including inter-chain contact map and interface residue recognition). 415 \nA key advantage of PPLM over existing language models lies in its attention mechanism, which 416 \ndisentangles intra- and inter-protein positional encoding while incorporating learnable inter -protein 417 \nweights and masking strategies. Coupled with training on a comprehensive paired -sequence dataset, 418 \nthese innovations allow PPLM to effectively model both intra - and inter-protein interaction patterns. 419 \nBenchmarking results show that PPLM achieves significantly lower perplexity than ESM2 on both 420 \ngeneral residues and interface regions, and its versatility across downstream tasks, combined with 421 \ncustomized architectures, leads to substantial performance improvements over existing approaches.  422 \nBy leveraging both learned embeddings and intra -/inter-protein attention matrices, PPLM -PPI 423 \nachieves substantial performance improvements in binary interaction prediction, surpassing existing 424 \nprotein language model-based methods by an average of over 11% across five test species. For binding 425 \naffinity prediction, fine-tuning the final layer of PPLM enables PPLM-Affinity to outperform ESM2-426 \nAffinity by more than 14% in terms of Pearson and Spearman correlations with experimental 427 \nmeasurements. Remarkably, on two biomedically critical subgroups  of antibody–antigen and TCR–428 \npMHC interactions, PPLM-Affinity achieves gains of 50% and 10%, respectively, over the best -429 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 10 \nperforming alternative methods. For inter -protein contact prediction, PPLM -Contact integrates the 430 \ninter-protein attention matrices derived from PPLM with a specialized transformer architecture, 431 \nachieving over 10% higher top  L precision on homodimers and more than 30% improvement on 432 \nheterodimers compared to existing approaches. When further enhanced with inter -protein distance 433 \nfeatures extracted from predicted complex structures, the extended model PPLM -Contact2 434 \nconsistently outperforms the cutting -edge structure-based methods , including AlphaFold2.339, 435 \nAlphaFold340, and DMFold41, in inter-chain contact prediction. 436 \nWhile this study demonstrates the effectiveness of PPLM across key PPI-related tasks, its potential 437 \napplications extend beyond the scope evaluated here. One promising direction involves integrating 438 \nPPLM’s interface -aware representations with AI-driven structural prediction tools such as 439 \nAlphaFold340 to improve the modeling of protein complex assemblies. In addition, the transferability 440 \nof PPLM to antibody–antigen and TCR–pMHC systems merits further exploration. Given its lower 441 \nperplexity at interaction interfaces relative to existing language models, PPLM may serve as a valuable 442 \nfoundation for complementarity-determining region (CDR) modeling and design. Its ability to capture 443 \nfine-grained interface features may support the accurate modeling of binding interactions, thereby 444 \nfacilitating the development and optimization of therapeutic antibodies and TCRs. 445 \nDespite its strong performance, PPLM has some limitations that warrant further investigation. Its 446 \ntraining relies on a composite PPI dataset that, while diverse, may not fully capture the breadth of 447 \nprotein interactions across organisms and cellular states.  In particular, transient, weak, or condition -448 \nspecific interactions remain underrepresented, potentially limiting generalizability to less common PPI 449 \nmodes. Moreover, PPLM focuses primarily on modeling sequence -derived representations, without 450 \nexplicitly incorporating biophysical constraints or functional annotations. Incorporating 451 \ncomplementary signals—such as thermodynamics, mutational effects, or co-evolutionary cues—may 452 \nfurther improve predictive accuracy and interpretability, particularly for context -specific or 453 \nmechanistically complex interactions. 454 \n 455 \nMethods 456 \nDatasets 457 \nTo train PPLM, we constructed a composite protein dataset of protein interaction sequence pair by 458 \nintegrating protein complex structures from the Protein Data Bank (PDB) 37 and PPI sequences from 459 \nthe STRING 38 database. From the PDB, we extracted high -quality sequence pairs from protein 460 \ncomplexes released before January 1, 2024, selecting only those in which at least one pair of residues 461 \nfrom different chains had a Cβ–Cβ distance within 8Å. This yielded 273,295 heteromeric and 86,915 462 \nhomomeric sequence pairs. These pairs are clustered using MMseqs2 46 at a 50% pairwise sequence 463 \nidentity threshold with 80% alignment coverage, resulting in 25,245 heteromeric and 23,082 464 \nhomomeric clusters. To further expand the diversity and coverage of interaction types, we incorporated 465 \nhigh-confidence interaction pairs from the STRING database, which contains over one billion protein–466 \nprotein associations. For this, we selected 2,995,604 sequence pairs with the highest confidence scores 467 \n(>0.999) and clustered them with the same sequence identity cutoff and coverage criteria used for 468 \nclustering the PDB-derived pairs. Clusters redundant with those from the PDB were removed, yielding 469 \n629,045 clusters. For model validation, we randomly sampled one representative pair from each of 470 \n1,306 heteromeric, 1,194 homomeric, and 2,500 STRING-derived clusters to construct the validation 471 \nset. The remaining clusters were used as the training set. 472 \nTo train and evaluate PPLM-PPI for PPI prediction, we adopted benchmark datasets from D -473 \nSCRIPT15 spanning six species: H. sapiens, M. musculus, D. melanogaster, C. elegans, S. cerevisiae, 474 \nand E. coli. Given the relative rarity of true PPIs, each dataset maintains a 10:1 ratio of negative to 475 \npositive pairs. To ensure data integrity, we identified and removed duplicate, erroneous, and invalid 476 \nsamples arising from the random negative sampling process (see Text S1 and Figure S7). The PPLM-477 \nPPI model was trained and validated on the H. sapiens dataset and tested on the remaining five species 478 \ndatasets. Detailed dataset statistics are listed in Table S12. 479 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 11 \nFor protein–protein binding affinity prediction, we adopted the PPB-Affinity dataset27, a large and 480 \ncurated resource compiled from multiple public affinity databases. The dataset provides 481 \nexperimentally determined binding affinities, crystal structures, mutation patterns, and annotations of 482 \nreceptor and ligand chains. It comprises 12,062 interaction samples from 3,032 distinct PDB entries, 483 \nencompassing subgroups such as antibody–antigen and TCR–pMHC complexes. Notably, 24.3% of 484 \nentries feature single -point mutants, while 21.1% include multiple mutation types, offering broad 485 \ncoverage of mutational landscapes relevant to affinity modeling.  PPLM-Affinity was trained and 486 \nevaluated using  five-fold cross -validation, following the benchmark protocol evaluation settings 487 \nestablished in the original PPB-Affinity study. 488 \nFor inter-protein contact prediction, we adopted the same datasets as DeepInter 20 to ensure fair 489 \ncomparison with existing methods. These datasets consist of non -redundant homodimers and 490 \nheterodimers curated from the PDB. The training set includes 3,504 homodimers and 1,881 491 \nheterodimers, while the validation set comprises 296 homodimers and 96 heterodimers . The test set 492 \ncontain 300 homodimers (Homodimer300) and 99 heterodimers (Heterodimer99). To account for 493 \nstructural differences between homodimers and heterodimers, we trained separate models: the 494 \nhomodimer model s was trained exclusively on homodimers, whereas the heterodimer model was 495 \ntrained on the full combined training set. To further assess robustness and real-world applicability, we 496 \nassessed PPLM -Contact on an independent test set comprising 43 homodimer targets 497 \n(CASP_Homodimer43) and 20 heterodimer targets (CASP_Heterodimer20) collected from CASP13 498 \nto CASP16 (see Supplementary Data 1 and 2). 499 \n 500 \nProtein–protein language model 501 \nPPLM is a pretrained protein–protein language model that can capture potential protein interaction 502 \ninformation by providing informative representations of protein pairs (Figure 1A). Given a protein 503 \nsequence pair as input, the  sequences are first tokenized independently and then concatenated with 504 \nspecial beginning -of-sequence (BOS) and end -of-sequence (EOS) tokens to explicitly mark the 505 \nboundaries of each chain. The concatenated initial representations will be used as the input of a stack 506 \nof 33 serially connected transformer blocks, with each block consisting of a tailored multi -head 507 \nattention module and a feed-forward network.  508 \nIn the multi -head attention module, we introduce a novel attention mechanism to capture both  509 \nintra- and inter-protein residue interactions. To distinguish these two interaction types , two separate 510 \nattention matrices are computed: one with rotary positional embeddings (RoPE )36 for intra -chain 511 \nresidue pairs, and another without positional encoding  (ROW) for inter-protein residue pairs. These 512 \ntwo matrices are then integrated into a full-size attention matrix. To enhance PPLM’s ability to capture 513 \ndiverse inter -protein interaction patterns , we introduce  a set of learnable weights  ( 𝑊!\" ) across 514 \ntransformer layers (𝑙) and attention heads (ℎ), applied specifically to the  inter-protein attention 515 \ncomponents. Additionally, an inter-protein binary mask (𝑀#$) is employed to distinguish inter-protein 516 \nfrom intra-chain residues pairs (i, j) and to better guide the model’s focus on inter-protein interactions. 517 \nThus, the full attention matrix is defined as: 518 \n𝐴!\"#$ = 𝑊!\" ⋅ 𝑀#$ ⋅ 𝐴!\"#$\n%&' + 21 − 𝑀#$ 4 ⋅ 𝐴!\"#$\n%&() + 𝑀#$ \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(1) 519 \nwhere 𝐴!\"#$\n%&' and 𝐴!\"#$\n%&()  represent the attention matrices computed without and with RoPE, respectively, 520 \nand 𝑀#$ is a binary mask matrix with value 1 for inter-protein residue pairs and 0 for intra-protein pairs. 521 \nThe masking strategy plays a pivotal role in masked language model training. In PPLM, we adopt 522 \ndistinct masking strategies for sequence pairs derived from different data resources . For high-quality 523 \nsequence pairs  from the PDB  database, greater emphasis is placed on  interface residues:  30% of 524 \ninterface residues are randomly masked, compared to 15% of non-interface residues. In contrast, for 525 \nsequence pairs from the STRING database , a uniform masking strategy is applied, where 15% of 526 \nresidues in each sequence are randomly selected for masking. To accommodate GPU memory 527 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 12 \nlimitations, sequence pair with full length exceeding 1,024 residues are cropped. For sequence pairs 528 \nfrom PDB database , cropping is biased toward retaining fragments include most interface residues . 529 \nFor sequence pairs from  STRING database, continuous fragment s are cropped from each sequence, 530 \nwith fragment lengths proportional to the original sequence lengths. 531 \nThe final objective function is a weighted average of cross-entropy losses computed separately 532 \nover masked residues in each sequence: 533 \nℒ*+* = − 1\n|𝑀,| : log𝑝(𝑥#\n(,)|𝑥\\𝑀) − 1\n|𝑀/|\t : log𝑝(𝑥#\n(/)|𝑥\\𝑀)\n#∈*!#∈*\"\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(2) 534 \nwhere 𝑥(,)  and 𝑥(/)  represent the amino acid sequences of the two protein s, and  𝑥 = (𝑥(,), 𝑥(/)) 535 \nrepresents all residues from both sequences. 𝑀, and 𝑀/ denote the sets of masked residue positions in 536 \neach sequence, and 𝑀 = 𝑀, ⋃ 𝑀/\tdenotes all masked positions across both sequences. The notation 537 \n𝑥\\𝑀 refers to the unmasked residues from both sequences. 538 \nDuring the training procedure, sequence pairs are sampled from the PDB and STRING clusters at 539 \na 1:2 ratio. PPLM was initialized from the 650M -parameter ESM2 model 6 and trained using four 540 \nNVIDIA A100 GPUs with a gradient accumulation step of 32 , totaling 6.4 million training steps. 541 \nOptimization was performed using the AdamW optimizer with  exponential decay rates β1=0.9 and 542 \nβ2=0.98. The learning rate was linearly warmed up to 1×10⁻⁶ over the first 2,000 steps , followed by a 543 \nlinear decay to 5×10⁻⁷ over the remainder of the training. 544 \n 545 \nInteraction prediction using PPLM 546 \nBuilding upon PPLM, we developed PPLM -PPI for protein–protein interaction prediction by 547 \nleveraging the protein pair representations generated by PPLM ( Figure 1B). Given a pair of input 548 \nprotein sequences, PPLM is first used to extract several features: the embeddings of each sequence, 549 \nthe intra-protein attention matrices for both sequences, and the inter-protein attention matrix between 550 \nsequences. For each feature, max pooling and mean pooling are independently applied along the 551 \nsequence dimension to obtain compact representations. These representations are then projected into 552 \na unified dimensional space using separate linear layers  and subsequently concatenated into a single 553 \njoint representation. A n MLP, followed by a sigmoid activation layer, is then used to predict the 554 \ninteraction probability. The MLP consists of five linear layers, with each of the first four layers 555 \nfollowed by layer normalization and a ReLU activation. The dimensionality parameters used at each 556 \ntransformation step are summarized in Tables S13–14. 557 \nPPLM-PPI was trained using 10 -fold cross -validation on the H. sapiens  dataset. The data was 558 \nrandomly divided into ten subsets, ensuring a balanced ratio of positive and negative samples in each. 559 \nFor each fold, one subset was held out for validation, while the remaining nine were used for training. 560 \nModels were trained for 12 epochs per fold, and the checkpoint with the highest AUPRC on the 561 \nvalidation set was selected. The five best-performing models across all folds were then ensembled to 562 \ngenerate final predictions during inference on the test sets. Training was performed on a single 563 \nNVIDIA A100 GPU with a batch size of 32. The model was optimized using the AdamW optimizer 564 \nwith a learning rate of 5×10⁻⁵. The loss function was binary cross -entropy between predicted 565 \nprobabilities and ground-truth interaction labels, defined as: 566 \nℒ112 = −(𝑦 ∙ log𝑝 + (1 − 𝑦) ∙ log(1 − 𝑝))\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(3) 567 \nwhere 𝑦 ∈ {0, 1} denotes the ground-truth interaction label, with 1 for positive and 0 for negative, and 568 \n𝑝 ∈ (0, 1) is the predicted probability of interaction. 569 \n 570 \nBinding affinity prediction using PPLM 571 \nWe further developed a sequence -based method, PPLM -Affinity, for protein–protein binding 572 \naffinity prediction by fine-tuning the final layer (i.e., the last transformer block) of PPLM on a curated 573 \naffinity dataset (Figure 1C). Given the limited size of the dataset —many samples involve mutations 574 \nand include multi -chain receptor or ligand proteins, especially within antibody–antigen and TCR–575 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 13 \npMHC subgroups—we fine-tuned the final layer to better adapt PPLM’s contextual representations for 576 \ncapturing sequence-level determinants of molecular interaction strength. The full -length embeddings 577 \noutput by the fine-tuned PPLM are aggregated along the sequence dimension using max pooling, and 578 \nthe resulting vector is passed through two fully connected layers with an intermediate ReLU activation 579 \nto predict the binding affinity value. In cases where the receptor or ligand consists of multiple chains, 580 \ntheir sequences are concatenated into a single sequence prior to input into PPLM. 581 \nPPLM-Affinity was trained and evaluated using five-fold cross-validation. To prevent data leakage, 582 \nsamples sharing the same PDB ID were grouped into the same fold. Each fold was trained for 15 583 \nepochs on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU. Optimizat ion was performed using the AdamW optimizer 584 \nwith a learning rate of 1×10⁻⁴, and the loss function was the mean squared error (MSE) between 585 \npredicted and experimentally determined binding affinities: 586 \nℒ344#5#67 = 1\n𝑁 :(𝑦K# − 𝑦#)/\n8\n#9,\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(3) 587 \nwhere 𝑦# and 𝑦K# represent the ground-truth and predicted binding affinity, respectively; N is the batch 588 \nsize, which equals 1 in our setting. 589 \n 590 \nInter-protein contact prediction using PPLM 591 \nIn addition to predicting interaction and binding strength, we further developed PPLM-Contact for 592 \ninter-protein contact prediction and identify interaction interfaces. As illustrated in Figure 1D, PPLM-593 \ncontact consists of two main components: (1) feature extraction from PPLM, MSA, and monomer 594 \nstructures; and (2) a novel inter -protein transformer architecture incorporating parallel triangle 595 \nmultiplication, cross-attention and self-attention mechanisms. 596 \nFor each input protein pair, features are extracted from three sources. First, PPLM is used to create 597 \nthe inter-protein attention matrix, which captures cross-chain dependencies and serves as a key inter-598 \nprotein feature. Second, MSA s for each monomer sequence is collected by searching against the 599 \nUniRef30_2021_0347 database using HHblits48 with hyperparameters set to ‘-e 1e-3 -cov 0.4 -id 99’. 600 \nFor heterodimers, paired MSAs are constructed based on species annotation41, while for homodimers, 601 \nthe monomer MSA is directly reused as the paired MSA. A variety of features are then extracted from 602 \nboth monomer and paired MSAs, including position-specific scoring matrices (PSSMs)49, row scores 603 \nand average product corrections from direct coupling analysis (DCA) 50, as well as MSA embeddings 604 \nand attention matrices derived from ESM-MSA-1b51. Lastly, the monomer distance maps are extracted 605 \nfrom either experimentally determined  (when available)  or predicted monomer structures  (by 606 \nAlphaFold252). The dimensions of all features are detailed in Table S15. For the enhanced version, 607 \nPPLM-Contact2, inter -protein distance map extracted from the complex structure predicted by 608 \nAlphaFold2.3 is incorporated as an additional feature. 609 \nIn the inter-protein transformer architecture, two ResNet modules are used to independently encode 610 \nintra-protein and inter-protein features before passing into the inter-protein transformer blocks (Figure 611 \nS8). The intra-protein features of both sequences  are processed by a shared  intra-protein ResNet 612 \nmodule with tied parameters. This is followed by 12 inter-protein transformer blocks, each containing 613 \ntwo parallel triangle multiplication modules, two parallel cross -attention modules, two parallel self -614 \nattention modules, and a transition layer (see middle box of Figure 1D). 615 \nThe triangle multiplication module is designed to update the inter -protein pair representation by 616 \nintegrating information from both intra- and inter-protein pairs (Figure S9). Specifically, for a given 617 \ninter-protein residue pair 𝑧#$, the module aggregates signals from an intermediate residue k, utilizing 618 \nintra-chain interactions 𝑟#:  and cross -chain interactions 𝑧:$ , while ensuring geometric triangle 619 \nconstraints are maintained. A parallel operation is simultaneously applied to both proteins, allowing 620 \ninter-protein features to be updated  using intra -protein information from each chain . The detailed 621 \nupdate equations are: 622 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 14 \n𝑧#$\n; = ℒ2𝑧#$ 4 ⊙ 𝜎 Oℒ2𝑧#$4P ;\t𝑟#:\n; = ℒ(𝑟#: ) ⊙ 𝜎2ℒ(𝑟#:)4;\t\t𝑔#$ = 𝜎 Oℒ2𝑧#$ 4P\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(4) 623 \n𝑧̃#$ = 𝑔#$ ⊙ 𝜑 V: 𝑟#:\n; ⋅ 𝑧#$\n;\n+\n:9,\nW\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(5) 624 \nwhere 𝑧#$  and 𝑟#:  are the inter - and intra -protein pair representations, respectively, and 𝑧̃#$  is the 625 \nupdated 𝑧#$ based on one protein; ℒ presents a linear transformation, 𝜎 the sigmoid activation, and 𝜑\ta 626 \nLayerNorm followed by a linear transformation. L is the monomer sequence length. Two  triangle 627 \nmultiplication operations, with one per protein, are performed in parallel, and the resulting outputs are 628 \ncombined to update the original 𝑧#$. 629 \nThe cross -attention module is designed to update the inter -protein pair representation 𝑧#$  by 630 \nattending to all intra-protein pair 𝑟#5 that share residue i (or 𝑟$5 that share residue j), thereby capturing 631 \nrich contextual information from the corresponding monomer protein (Figure S10). In this mechanism, 632 \nthe inter-protein pair representation acts as the query, while the intra-protein pairs serve as the key and 633 \nvalue in the attention operation, i.e., 634 \n𝑞#$\n\" = ℒ2𝑧#$ 4;\t\t𝑘#5\n\" = ℒ(𝑟#5);\t𝑣#5\n\" = ℒ(𝑟#5); 𝑏#$\n\" = ℒ2𝑧#$4; 𝑔#$\n\" = 𝜎 Oℒ2𝑧#$ 4P\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(6) 635 \n𝑎#$:\n\" = softmax d 1\n√𝑐 𝑞#$\n\" ⋅ 𝑘#5\n\" + 𝑏#$\n\" g\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(7) 636 \n𝑧̃#$ = ℒ V𝑔#$\n\" ⊙ : 𝑎#$:\n\" ⋅ 𝑣#5\n\"\n+\n59,\nW\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(8) 637 \nTwo parallel cross-attention operations are performed across two proteins according to the equations 638 \nabove, and the resulting updates are then combined to update the inter-protein pair representation. 639 \nA self-attention module is applied to further update the inter-protein representation from its own 640 \ncontext (Figure S11). Specifically, it attends over all inter-protein pairs 𝑧#5 that share residue i (or 𝑧$5 641 \nthat share residue j) to update 𝑧#$, thereby enabling long-range information flow across the protein 642 \ninterface. During this process, intra -protein distances  extracted from monomer structure  are 643 \ntransformed using a Gaussian kernel and used to modulate the attention weights 20. The detailed 644 \nformulation is: 645 \n𝑞#$\n\" = ℒ2𝑧#$ 4;\t𝑘#5\n\" = ℒ(𝑧#5);\t𝑣#5\n\" = ℒ(𝑧#5);\t𝑔#$\n\" = 𝜎 Oℒ2𝑧#$ 4P\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(9) 646 \n𝑎#$:\n\" = softmax d 1\n√𝑐 𝑞#$\n\" ⋅ 𝑘#5\n\" ⋅ 𝑑#5g\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(10) 647 \n𝑧̃#$ = ℒ V𝑔#$\n\" ⊙ : 𝑎#$:\n\" ⋅ 𝑣#5\n\"\n+\n5<,\nW\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(11) 648 \nwhere din denotes the intra-protein distance transformed by the Gaussian kernel function. Parallel self-649 \nattention operations are performed independently along two monomer protein dimensions and then 650 \naggregated to update the inter-protein pair representation 𝑧#$. Finally, a transition layer consisting of 651 \ntwo linear transformations is applied at the end of each transformer block. 652 \nPPLM-Contact was trained on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU for a total of 100 epoch using the 653 \nAdamW optimizer. The initial learning rate was set to 1×10⁻ 3 and decayed by a factor of 0.98 after 654 \neach epoch. To handle long sequences during training, monomer proteins longer than 256 residues 655 \nwere cropped by randomly selecting a contiguous 256 -residue fragment that contained the highest 656 \nnumber of interface residues. Inter -protein residue pairs were defined as contacts if the minimum 657 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 15 \ndistance between their heavy atoms was less than 8Å 53. The top five models with the highest top L 658 \ninter-protein contact precision on the validation set were  then used for ensemble prediction during 659 \ninference. Given the severe class imbalance between contact and non -contact residue pairs, we 660 \nemployed the Focal Loss function54 to mitigate the influence of dominant non-contact pairs:  661 \nℒ=&56>?6 = − 1\n𝐿,𝐿/\n: : 𝛼21 − 𝑝#$4\n@\n+!\n$9,\n+\"\n#9,\nlog\t(𝑝#$ )\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(12) 662 \nwhere 𝑝#$ is the predicted contact probability; 𝛼 = 0.25 is a balance factor, and 𝛾 = 1.5 is a focusing 663 \nfactor; 𝐿, and 𝐿/ are the protein lengths of the two monomer proteins, respectively. 664 \n 665 \nData availability 666 \nThe dataset used in this study is deposited at both https://zhanggroup.org/PPLM/benchmark.html 667 \nand our GitHub repository: https://github.com/junliu621/PPLM/tree/main/data/. Protein pairs used to 668 \ntrain PPLM are collected from  the Protein Data Bank  (PDB, https://www.rcsb.org/) and STRING 669 \ndatabase (https://string-db.org/). The interaction dataset for PPLM -PPI is sourced from D -SCRIPT 670 \n(https://github.com/samsledje/D-SCRIPT/), while the binding affinity dataset for PPLM -Affinity is 671 \ntaken from PPB -Affinity ( https://github.com/ChenPy00/PPB-Affinity). The Uniref30_2021_03 672 \ndatabase is available at https://wwwuser.gwdguser.de/~compbiol/uniclust/. 673 \n 674 \nCode availability 675 \nThe PPLM webserver and source codes, including the language model, interaction prediction, 676 \nbinding affinity prediction, and inter -protein contact prediction, are freely accessible at 677 \nhttps://zhanggroup.org/PPLM/. The source codes are also available on GitHub at 678 \nhttps://github.com/junliu621/PPLM. 679 \n 680 \nAcknowledgements 681 \nWe thank Dr. Yang Li for discussions. This work is supported in part by Singapore Ministry of 682 \nEducation (T1 251RES2309), National University of Singapore Startup Grants (#A -8001129-00-00, 683 \n#A-0009651-30-00), Cancer Science Institute of Singapore, and Natural Science Foundation of 684 \nNingxia Province 2023AAC05036. The funders had no role in study design, data collection and 685 \nanalysis, decision to publish or preparation of the manuscript. 686 \n 687 \nAuthor contributions 688 \nY .Z. conceived and designed the project and supervised the work. J.L. developed the methods and 689 \nperformed. J.L and H.C. prepared the initial data, performed the benchmark and analyzed the data. J.L 690 \nand Y .Z wrote the paper. All authors wrote the manuscript and approved the final version. 691 \n 692 \nReference 693 \n1. Barabasi, A. -L. & Oltvai, Z.N. Network biology: understanding the cell's functional 694 \norganization. Nature reviews genetics 5, 101-113 (2004). 695 \n2. Keskin, O., Gursoy, A., Ma, B. & Nussinov, R. Principles of protein− protein interactions: what 696 \nare the preferred ways for proteins to interact? Chemical reviews 108, 1225-1244 (2008). 697 \n3. Siebenmorgen, T. & Zacharias, M. Computational prediction of protein –protein binding 698 \naffinities. Advanced Review 10, e1448 (2019). 699 \n4. Vangone, A. & Bonvin, A.M. Contacts-based prediction of binding affinity in protein–protein 700 \ncomplexes. elife 4, e07454 (2015). 701 \n5. Rives, A. et al. Biological structure and function emerge from scaling unsupervised learning to 702 \n250 million protein sequences. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences  118, 703 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 16 \ne2016239118 (2021). 704 \n6. Lin, Z. et al. Evolutionary -scale prediction of atomic -level protein structure with a language 705 \nmodel. Science 379, 1123-1130 (2023). 706 \n7. Hayes, T. et al. Simulating 500 million years of evolution with a language model. bioRxiv, 707 \n2024.2007. 2001.600583 (2024). 708 \n8. Elnaggar, A. et al. Prottrans: Toward u