put 821 \nsequences, PPLM is used to generate the embeddings, intra-protein attention matrices and inter-protein attention 822 \nmatrix, which are aggregated using max and mean pooling. The aggregated features are concatenated and passed 823 \nthrough a multilayer perceptron to predict the interaction probability between the input sequences. (C) PPLM-824 \nAffinity pipeline. The final transformer block of PPLM is finetuned on binding affinity data, where the resulting 825 \nembeddings for paired sequences are aggregated via max pooling and passed through two fully connected layers 826 \nto predict binding affinity value. (D) PPLM-Contact pipeline. For a given sequence pairs, inter-protein attention 827 \nmatrix from PPLM is integrated with MSA -derived features and monomer distance maps to capture both 828 \nevolutionary and structural information. These features are processed by a series of inter -protein transformer 829 \nblocks, with each composed of three core modules to predict the inter-protein contacts. Each core module adopts 830 \na parallel architecture to update inter-protein representations using both intra- and inter-protein features.  831 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 20 \n 832 \nFigure 2. Perplexity of PPLM and ESM2 on protein sequence pairs. (A) Perplexity distribution for 833 \nsequence pairs from the  PDB and STRING databases under random masking and interface masking 834 \nstrategies. Interface masking is applied only to sequence pairs from PDB database, with single-chain 835 \ninterface masking used exclusively for heterodimers . (B) Head-to-head comparison of perplexity  836 \nbetween PPLM and ESM2  under random masking . (C) Head-to-head comparison under overall 837 \ninterface masking, including both dual-chain and single-chain masking. 838 \n  839 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 21 \n 840 \nFigure 3. Performance of PPLM-PPI and PPLM-Affinity on protein–protein interaction and 841 \nbinding affinity prediction. (A) AUPRC of PPLM-PPI, ESMDNN-PPI, D-SCRIPT, and Topsy-Turvy 842 \nacross five species test sets.  (B) AUROC of the four PPI prediction methods on the five species test 843 \nsets. (C) Precision-recall curves of the four PPI prediction methods on the five species test sets.  (D) 844 \nPearson (PCC) and Spearman (SRCC) correlation coefficients of PPLM-Affinity, ESM2-Affinity, and 845 \nPPB-Affinity evaluated on the entire dataset, as well as the antibody–antigen and TCR–pMHC 846 \nsubgroups. (E) Comparison between experimental and PPLM-Affinity predicted binding affinities on 847 \nthe entire datase t. (F) Comparison between experimental and ESM2 -Affinity predicted binding 848 \naffinities on the entire dataset. 849 \n  850 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 22 \n 851 \nFigure 4. Performance of PPLM -Contact and the comparison methods on the inter -protein 852 \ncontact prediction. (A) Top L contact precision of PPLM-Contact, DeepInter, CDPred, DeepHomo2.0, 853 \nand GLINTER on the Homodimer300 and CASP_Homodimer43 test sets using both experimental 854 \nmonomer structures and AlphaFold2 -predicted monomer structures. (B) Top L contact precision of 855 \nPPLM-Cxontact, DeepInter, CDPred, and GLINTER on the Heterodimer99 and 856 \nCASP_Heterodimer20 test sets using both experimental monomer structures and AlphaFold2 -857 \npredicted monomer structures. (C) Head-to-head comparison of top L contact precision between 858 \nPPLM-Contact and other methods on the 343 homodimer and 119 heterodimer proteins . (D) Top 859 \ncontact precision s of PPLM -Contact and its ablation variants on the Homodimer300 and 860 \nHeterodimer99 test sets. (E) An example of inter -protein contact prediction for the EF-hand domain 861 \nof human RASEF  (PDB ID: 2PMY). (F) Contact maps for 2PMY predicted by PPLM -Contact w/o 862 \nPPLM, PPLM-Contact w/o MSA, and the full PPLM-Contact models, respectively. Each contact map 863 \nshows the alignment between ground-truth contacts and top N predicted contacts, where N equals the 864 \nnumber of ground-truth contacts. Grey dots representing ground-truth contacts, red dots indicate true 865 \npositive predictions, and blue dots denote false positive predictions. The number in the parentheses 866 \ndenotes the top N contact precision. 867 \n  868 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 23 \n 869 \nFigure 5. Comparative analysis of protein–protein interface residue identification. (A) Precision 870 \nof interface residue identification  by PPLM -Contact, DeepInter, CDPred , DeepHomo2.0, and 871 \nGLINTER on the homodimer and heterodimer proteins  in the test sets using experimental monomer 872 \nstructures. (B) Head-to-head comparison of identification precision between PPLM-Contact and each 873 \n